{"address_mailing":"PO Box 728 Burns OR 97720","mailing_city":"Burns","mailing_zipcode":"97720","mailing_addressType":"Postal","contact_first_name":"Angela","contact_last_name":"Lamborn","essay_community_support":"The box truck would be available to move medical equipment and other necessary household items in our rural frontier community.  It will have all the carpentry tools available and needed to make minor and necessary repairs to homes of those affected by disaster.  It would also be available to transport product to frontier communities in the county and could be put into use hauling sand bags and other equipment needed to address the current disaster situation.

The box truck that would have the generator attached to it would be available to travel to affected frontier communities to assist with power needs.  An example would be parking it at a local gathering center such as a schoolhouse and provide power needed to that building to provide heat/cooling or run medical equipment for local residents in need.

The generator installed at the Harney Hub site would allow us to be the gathering center for Red Cross as agreed and have the power available to manage any situation.  It would allow us to run our kitchen and provide meals to partners and our community.  It would allow us to access our electronic information and serve those in need in the community through our lists and other information we hold in our database. ","essay_communication":"If we are awarded the grant we will share our improvements of services in our newsletter, a letter in the paper and an interview on the local radio station.  We will also communicate the availability of the mobile unit to the emergency management director so she is aware the resource is available.  We will also reaffirm the building availability as a staging site or shelter site if we are awarded the generator."}

{"address_mailing":"1106 K Ave, La Grande, OR, 97850, USA","mailing_street":"1106 K Ave","mailing_city":"La Grande","mailing_zipcode":"97850","mailing_addressType":"PointAddress","contact_first_name":"Nick","contact_last_name":"Vora","essay_community_support":"Union County Emergency Services has conducted several exercises in recent years that have consistently identified sheltering capacity as a substantial and high priority capability gap in Union County. This project (typed packages) will allow Union County agency staff and authorized and trained volunteers to rapidly open and staff a shelter or shelters in response to an emergency using local and immediately available supplies. This is not a capability that currently exists in functional form in Union County. 

Having a functional emergency sheltering and mass care program will make the community significantly more resilient to the disturbance of an emergency, as people will be able to have their basic needs met locally, by local staff. At our present levels of capability, it could take many hours or longer to open a shelter in Union County, and we would require mutual aid from neighboring jurisdictions. An event that highlighted the criticality of this situation was an electrical outage on a very cold night in January when nearly half the population of Union County lost electricity.  Fortunately service was restored quickly, otherwise the hours that it would have taken to acquire generators and transport supplies to a shelter site would have taken precious hours when temperatures inside poorly insulated homes and trailers were dropping precipitously. This project will equip us with a majority of the tools and equipment items necessary to be able to rapidly respond to a range of emergencies ranging from a short-duration event like a power outage on an extremely cold night to a devastating regional earthquake. 
","essay_communication":"If awarded, Union County Emergency Services (UCES) will advertise the acquired resources to the members of the community, partner organizations, and neighboring jurisdictions. Local media will be invited to view the installation of the kits, and UCES will produce a press release and social media posts communicating the outcome of efforts done between ODHS-OREM and local organizations to increase local resilience and emergency response capabilities. UCES will continue to conduct and advertise sheltering training and exercises in the community using and testing the readiness of this equipment in partnership with local ODHS-OREM staff. "}

{"address_mailing":"323 N F St, Lakeview, OR, 97630, USA","mailing_street":"323 N F St","mailing_city":"Lakeview","mailing_zipcode":"97630","mailing_addressType":"PointAddress","contact_first_name":"Rob","contact_last_name":"Thornton","essay_community_support":"To determine the fitness of the Elks Lodge as a resilience hub, the local OHDS Emergency Manager, Rhonda Nyseth, has done an assessment of the Elks Lodge and determined it meets the criteria for a resilience hub. Additionally, the Red Cross has evaluated and approved the property as appropriate for them to use as a shelter in times of an emergency. The Elks Lodge checks many boxes necessary for a hub: it has sufficient floor space to house people, is ADA accessible, has a large kitchen for mass feeding, and is next door to the Lakeview fire station and Lake County EMS dispatch. For these reasons, The Elks Lodge can serve as an emergency shelter for people whose homes are uninhabitable for any reason, be a central location for sharing information with the community, and a place to distribute food, water, and other supplies as they become available. 

Building a solar plus storage system at the Elks Lodge would allow it to remain functioning and climate controlled in a disaster that knocks out the local power. It is extremely important that there is a designated Red Cross shelter facility with backup power in the area, because due to its isolation there could be an extended period of time before that power is restored. With further investment into a resilience network, the Elks Lodge could also serve as a shelter for people in the communities surrounding Lakeview. There are not many towns in Lake County large enough to justify investing in a resilience hub. Therefore, it is important that if one is built in Lakeview, there is further investment in spreading information and creating a network to respond to emergency situations and ensure nearby communities also have access to the hub.
","essay_communication":"The Lakeview Elks Lodge has several means of community outreach that will be leveraged to communicate the outcome of this funding request with members and the public. These methods include a monthly newsletter, coordination with Emergency Management Services to notify public and government entities, and a reader board. Additionally, the Lodge has agreed to notify local newspaper and radio about the project if awarded, communicating the ability for the facility to provide shelter in the event of emergency. "}
